course: dictionary
lesson: 621
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can mean different things, and the context tells you which meaning the writer wants. The word babbling is a good example. «Stop babbling and tell me what happened!» Here babbling means talking quickly in a silly, excited or confusing way, so the speaker is hard to follow.
explain: The same word can describe something very different. «The babbling brook ran over the stones.» No one is talking here. A brook makes a soft, bubbling sound as water runs over stones, so babbling describes that sound.
explain: Ask yourself who or what is doing the babbling: a person, a baby or a stream? The subject gives you the clue. Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Babbling can be a verb (she was babbling), an adjective (a babbling brook) or a noun (the babbling of the brook).
fact: «Babbling» means talking quickly in a silly, excited or confusing way.
fact: Babies babble with sounds like «ba-ba» and «da-da». This is practice, usually starting around six months, before they can say real words.
fact: A babbling brook makes a soft, bubbling, continuous sound as water runs over stones.
fact: In a story, a babbling character is often nervous or over-excited.
exercise: «Stop babbling and tell me what happened!» What does babbling mean here? | Singing softly to a tune | Swimming in the river | Eating slowly | *Talking quickly in a confused, excited way
exercise: «The babbling brook ran over the stones.» What makes the brook babbling? | A loud crash of waves on the beach | A child talking to a teddy | Thunder rolling over the hills | *A soft, bubbling sound as water runs over stones
exercise: Around six months, many babies start babbling. What does babbling look like in a baby? | *Repeating sounds like «ba-ba» and «da-da», practice before real words | A song with a clear tune | A full sentence like «I want my milk now» | A quiet whisper into a pillow
test: «Her babbling about the party made no sense at all.» What does babbling suggest? | Singing a happy song | Whispering a secret | Writing a clear letter | *Talking in a confused, excited way that is hard to follow
test: Which sentence uses babbling to mean the sound a baby makes? | *The baby was babbling «ba-ba, da-da» in her cot | Stop babbling and tell me the story | Babbling has two b's in it | The brook was babbling over the rocks
exam: Read this: «The stream was babbling, and the baby was babbling too.» Why does the writer use the same word for both? | Babbling means both of them are shouting loudly | *Babbling can describe a bubbling sound and a baby's first sounds | Babbling means both of them are asleep | It is a spelling mistake
